{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>Peoples will serve you, etc.</b> Perhaps it is because a king does not impose a tax upon one who is close to him among the people. Something analogous to this is found in the time when Israel is subjugated to the nations of the world: the kings among them who are masters of Torah are exempt from the tax (Ezra 7:24). And what he said afterward (Genesis 27:40) ״וְאֶת אָחִיךָ תַּעֲבֹד״ — and he was a king — refers back to the sons of Esav." "<b>Be a lord over your brothers.</b> That is, his actual brother would not be a slave to him, but rather he would be a lord over him; but those who come after him would bow down to him. And this is what the verse says: <b>and let your mother's sons bow down to you</b>." "<b>Further,</b> it intends that he would prevail over him through the medium of sacred worship — then would your mother's sons, etc., bow down to him. For apart from this, one brother would not be subjugated to another. And therefore you will find that with even a slight relaxation that Israel would allow in the worship of HaShem, immediately and at once (II Kings 8:20) וַיִּפְשַׁע אֱדוֹם." "<b>Those who curse you, etc., and those who bless you, etc.</b> That is: those who curse you — in the future, cursed is he from now, and he will not arise to curse; and those who bless you — blessed is he from beforehand, so that his blessing may come upon you." "<b>And the reason</b> the verse says ״מְבָרְכֶיךָ״ in the plural, yet blessed only one — and likewise it mentions cursers in the plural yet ״cursed״ in the singular — is to exclude a portion of the cursers who are not cursed, namely Ahiyah the Shilonite (I Kings 11:29) and others like him. Therefore it says ״אָרוּר״ and not ״cursed ones״ — all of them. And among the blessers too there is one of the blessers who is not included in ״blessed,״ namely Bila'am the wicked, who even though he blessed, he is not blessed. Therefore it says ״מְבָרְכֶיךָ״ — there is among them one portion that is blessed, not all of them — to exclude the one mentioned." "And he said <b>those who bless you</b> with the addition of the letter vav, to allude to a kind of curser that is included within the category of blessing, and that is the one who intends to bless — as the Rabbis said to Rabbi Elazar son of Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai (Mo'ed Katan 9a): ״תִּזְרַע וְלֹא תֶחְצַד״ etc. And this is the measure of the verse <b>those who curse you are cursed, and those who bless you</b> — meaning: those who curse you — if they intend [to be] among those who bless you — are blessed."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:44:02.450162118Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}